## The Bridge Pattern in Java

The **Bridge** pattern is one of the 23 “Gang of Four” structural patterns.  
Its purpose is to **decouple an abstraction from its implementation** so that the two can evolve independently.  
In Java this is usually expressed with:

| Role | Typical Java construct | Responsibility |
|------|------------------------|----------------|
| **Abstraction** | `abstract class` | Defines the interface that clients use. Holds a reference to an *implementor*. |
| **RefinedAbstraction** | Subclass of the abstraction | Adds additional responsibilities. |
| **Implementor** | `interface` | Declares the methods that concrete implementors must provide. |
| **ConcreteImplementor** | Concrete class that implements the interface | Provides the actual work. |

> **Why use Bridge?**  
> - When you have two orthogonal dimensions (e.g., *shape* vs *color* or *device* vs *control*).  
> - When you need to vary implementations at runtime or on a per‑client basis.  
> - When you want to avoid a combinatorial explosion of subclasses.

---

## A Step‑by‑Step Java Implementation

Below is a classic “Device / Remote Control” example.  
You can easily adapt the same skeleton to shapes, printers, UI widgets, etc.

```java
// 1. Implementor
interface Device {
    void turnOn();
    void turnOff();
    void setChannel(int channel);
    void setVolume(int level);
}

// 2. ConcreteImplementors
class TV implements Device {
    @Override public void turnOn()   { System.out.println("TV is on"); }
    @Override public void turnOff()  { System.out.println("TV is off"); }
    @Override public void setChannel(int ch) { System.out.println("TV channel set to " + ch); }
    @Override public void setVolume(int lvl) { System.out.println("TV volume set to " + lvl); }
}

class Radio implements Device {
    @Override public void turnOn()   { System.out.println("Radio is on"); }
    @Override public void turnOff()  { System.out.println("Radio is off"); }
    @Override public void setChannel(int ch) { System.out.println("Radio channel set to " + ch); }
    @Override public void setVolume(int lvl) { System.out.println("Radio volume set to " + lvl); }
}

// 3. Abstraction
abstract class RemoteControl {
    protected Device device;          // Bridge reference

    public RemoteControl(Device dev) {
        this.device = dev;
    }

    public void togglePower() {
        // Default implementation – could be overridden
        // For the demo we just flip between on/off
    }

    public void setChannel(int ch) {
        device.setChannel(ch);
    }

    public void setVolume(int lvl) {
        device.setVolume(lvl);
    }
}

// 4. RefinedAbstraction
class BasicRemote extends RemoteControl {
    public BasicRemote(Device dev) { super(dev); }

    @Override public void togglePower() {
        // Simplest possible control: on->off or off->on
        System.out.println("BasicRemote: toggling power");
        // In a real implementation you would track state
    }
}

class AdvancedRemote extends RemoteControl {
    public AdvancedRemote(Device dev) { super(dev); }

    @Override public void togglePower() {
        System.out.println("AdvancedRemote: toggling power");
    }

    public void mute() {
        System.out.println("AdvancedRemote: muting device");
        device.setVolume(0);
    }
}

// 5. Client usage
public class BridgeDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Device tv = new TV();
        Device radio = new Radio();

        RemoteControl basicRemote = new BasicRemote(tv);
        RemoteControl advancedRemote = new AdvancedRemote(radio);

        basicRemote.togglePower();
        basicRemote.setChannel(5);
        basicRemote.setVolume(7);

        System.out.println("---");

        advancedRemote.togglePower();
        advancedRemote.setChannel(3);
        advancedRemote.setVolume(10);
        ((AdvancedRemote) advancedRemote).mute();
    }
}
```

### Output

```
BasicRemote: toggling power
TV channel set to 5
TV volume set to 7
---
AdvancedRemote: toggling power
Radio channel set to 3
Radio volume set to 10
AdvancedRemote: muting device
Radio volume set to 0
```

Notice how:

- The *abstraction* (`RemoteControl`) delegates the real work to the *implementor* (`Device`).
- You can swap the `Device` at runtime (`new TV()`, `new Radio()`).
- Adding a new remote (e.g., `SmartRemote`) doesn’t affect the devices, and vice‑versa.

